  "solution": {
    "text": "<p><img src=\"assets\\st0039_24c81d5b85c7.png\" style=\"width: 163px; height: 170px;\"/></p><p>Given the length of the pendulum is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>ℓ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn><mo> </mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></math>.</p><p>The initial potential energy of the pendulum at the horizontal position is given by</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>U</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>m</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>ℓ</mi><mo> </mo><mo> </mo><mo> </mo><mo>.</mo><mo>.</mo><mo>.</mo><mfenced><mn>1</mn></mfenced></math></p><p>The final kinetic energy of the pendulum at the lowermost position is given by</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mi>m</mi><msup><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo> </mo><mo> </mo><mo> </mo><mo>.</mo><mo>.</mo><mo>.</mo><mfenced><mn>2</mn></mfenced></math></p><p>So, from equations (1) and (2), it follows that</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mn>9</mn><mn>10</mn></mfrac><mi>m</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>ℓ</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mi>m</mi><msup><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mspace linebreak=\"newline\"></mspace><mo>⇒</mo><mfrac><mn>9</mn><mn>10</mn></mfrac><mo>×</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><msup><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mspace linebreak=\"newline\"></mspace><mo>⇒</mo><msup><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>180</mn><mspace linebreak=\"newline\"></mspace><mo>⇒</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mn>180</mn></msqrt><mspace linebreak=\"newline\"></mspace><mo>=</mo><mn>6</mn><msqrt><mn>5</mn></msqrt><mo> </mo><mo> </mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mo> </mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math></p>"
  }
